<div class="py-12 max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 space-y-8" data-page="faqs"><div class="text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto"><span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 uppercase tracking-widest">Practical Guidance</span><h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-black text-white font-display mt-1">Frequently Asked Questions</h1><p class="text-slate-400 mt-2 text-sm sm:text-base">Expert troubleshooting tailored for apartments, seasonal weather extremes, and municipal drop-offs.</p></div><div class="space-y-4"><div class="bg-white/[0.03] border border-white/10 rounded-3xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl"><button data-faq="0" class="w-full text-left p-6 font-extrabold text-base text-white flex items-center justify-between gap-4 cursor-pointer hover:bg-white/[0.03] transition font-display" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-answer-0"><span>Can I successfully compost on an apartment balcony in Ahmedabad or Surat?</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-chevron-down w-5 h-5 text-emerald-400 transition-transform duration-300 rotate-180" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path></svg></button><div class="p-6 pt-0 text-slate-300 text-sm leading-relaxed border-t border-white/5 font-light" id="faq-answer-0">Yes! Over 10,000 apartment residents across Gujarat compost successfully on balconies. Compact terracotta composters or airtight Bokashi systems occupy less than 2 sq ft. By keeping a 2:1 dry brown-to-green ratio, the pile stays sweet-smelling and free of fruit flies.</div></div><div class="bg-white/[0.03] border border-white/10 rounded-3xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl"><button data-faq="1" class="w-full text-left p-6 font-extrabold text-base text-white flex items-center justify-between gap-4 cursor-pointer hover:bg-white/[0.03] transition font-display" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-answer-1"><span>How does the Gujarat Municipal Composting Network work?</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-chevron-down w-5 h-5 text-emerald-400 transition-transform duration-300" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path></svg></button><div class="p-6 pt-0 text-slate-300 text-sm leading-relaxed border-t border-white/5 font-light" id="faq-answer-1" hidden="hidden">Municipal corporations across Gujarat (AMC, SMC, VMC, RMC) operate centralized bio-composting plants and community drop-off stations. If you cannot compost at home, you can segregate your wet organic scraps and deliver them to any of the 12 verified municipal drop-off hubs shown on our interactive map.</div></div><div class="bg-white/[0.03] border border-white/10 rounded-3xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl"><button data-faq="2" class="w-full text-left p-6 font-extrabold text-base text-white flex items-center justify-between gap-4 cursor-pointer hover:bg-white/[0.03] transition font-display" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-answer-2"><span>Can I compost citrus peels like lemon and orange skins in Gujarat?</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-chevron-down w-5 h-5 text-emerald-400 transition-transform duration-300" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path></svg></button><div class="p-6 pt-0 text-slate-300 text-sm leading-relaxed border-t border-white/5 font-light" id="faq-answer-2" hidden="hidden">Yes, in moderation! Citrus peelings contain natural d-limonene oil. When citrus makes up less than 15% of your total green volume and is sliced into smaller pieces, microbes digest it easily without hindering pile biology.</div></div><div class="bg-white/[0.03] border border-white/10 rounded-3xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl"><button data-faq="3" class="w-full text-left p-6 font-extrabold text-base text-white flex items-center justify-between gap-4 cursor-pointer hover:bg-white/[0.03] transition font-display" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-answer-3"><span>How do I protect my compost during Gujarat summer heatwaves (44°C+)?</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-chevron-down w-5 h-5 text-emerald-400 transition-transform duration-300" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path></svg></button><div class="p-6 pt-0 text-slate-300 text-sm leading-relaxed border-t border-white/5 font-light" id="faq-answer-3" hidden="hidden">Extreme summer heat evaporates moisture quickly. Keep your bin in shaded areas under balconies or shade nets. Lay a damp jute burlap sack directly over the top of the compost to trap moisture, and sprinkle water or diluted buttermilk once weekly.</div></div><div class="bg-white/[0.03] border border-white/10 rounded-3xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl"><button data-faq="4" class="w-full text-left p-6 font-extrabold text-base text-white flex items-center justify-between gap-4 cursor-pointer hover:bg-white/[0.03] transition font-display" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-answer-4"><span>What is "Compost Tea" and how do I harvest it?</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-chevron-down w-5 h-5 text-emerald-400 transition-transform duration-300" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path></svg></button><div class="p-6 pt-0 text-slate-300 text-sm leading-relaxed border-t border-white/5 font-light" id="faq-answer-4" hidden="hidden">Compost tea is a liquid microbial tonic. You can collect the leachate from bins with spigot taps, or steep a cup of cured compost in 5 liters of aerated water for 24 hours. Dilute with water (1:10 ratio) and spray directly on plant leaves or roots as organic fertilizer.</div></div></div></div>
